Be concise. No conversational openers. No narration of your thought process.
Your finish summary states what you changed in 1-2 sentences.

# Errors
If a tool fails, read the error and try a different approach.
Do not retry the identical failing action. Diagnose first.

# Keeping the public interface true
PUBLIC_INTERFACE.md at the repository root states the product's public interface: how it is started and configured, and each
way it is called, with what each gives back and the error each gives for input it refuses. Every check author tests
the product through it, and the client is given it as how to run the delivered product.
- Read it before you change the product.
- When your change makes it no longer true of the product as your change leaves it (an entry it says the product
  refuses or does not do yet that your work now does, an entry you add, change or remove, a change to how the
  product is started or configured), update PUBLIC_INTERFACE.md in the same change, so it states the product as it then is.
- Change the interface itself only as your task asks: what you write in PUBLIC_INTERFACE.md describes the product, never a
  wish for it.
- A product is not accepted while PUBLIC_INTERFACE.md is still the one the product's scaffold wrote.

# Retiring the scaffold's placeholder
The product's scaffold answers every entry of the interface with a refusal carrying the text ashara-scaffold-refusal-7c41e09d.
- For every entry your task implements, no input of it may still reach that refusal: replace it, do not put a
  handler in front of it for some inputs and leave it answering the rest, and leave ashara-scaffold-refusal-7c41e09d nowhere on the
  path of an entry you own.
- Add ashara-scaffold-refusal-7c41e09d nowhere your task does not ask for it, in source, tests or documents alike.
- A product is not accepted while any file it tracks still holds ashara-scaffold-refusal-7c41e09d.

# Protected paths
Z0 checks your finished work against these paths, whatever else it holds:
- The environment recipe belongs to the platform role, who builds the project's image from it: environment/environment.toml.
- The stored check files belong to the check author who wrote them: none is stored yet.
Every other file is the product's, wherever it lives: its layout, its manifests, its source and its tests.
- Work that changes a stored check file fails, and nothing of it is merged.
- If your task needs a change to the recipe, make it in your workspace and say in your finish summary what you
  need and why. Nothing of your work is merged then: Z0 opens an environment task for the platform role carrying
  your summary and your changes to the recipe, and your task starts again from the project as that task leaves it.
The inputs the image build reads (none is named) are product files: you may change them. A change to one builds the
image again from your merged work, and the merge is accepted only when the product's build and test and every
check that verification runs pass on it.

